        "reasoningContent" : "The user is asking about RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) - this is a standard concept in LLM applications. I should provide a clear, concise explanation with Java code examples as requested. This is a straightforward technical question that doesn't require any external tools or complex reasoning. Let me explain what RAG is and show how it's implemented in Java using common libraries like LangChain or similar frameworks.\n\nRAG stands for Retrieval-Augmented Generation. It's a pattern where:\n1. A retrieval component searches for relevant documents from a knowledge base\n2. The retrieved context is fed to the LLM as additional input\n3. The LLM generates an answer based on both its training data and the retrieved context\n\nKey components:\n- Vector store (for storing embeddings of documents)\n- Retriever (e.g., FAISS, Chroma, Weaviate)\n- Generator (LLM client)\n\nI'll provide a practical Java example using LangChain which is the most common framework for this."
      },
      "text" : "**RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)** is a pattern where you combine a large language model (LLM) with a retrievable knowledge source. Instead of relying solely on the model's pre-trained knowledge, RAG:\n\n1. **Retrieves** relevant documents from a knowledge base (using vector search/embeddings)\n2. **Feeds** those retrieved passages to the LLM as context\n3. **Generates** an answer that combines the model's internal knowledge + retrieved context\n\nThis solves the \"knowledge cutoff\" problem by grounding responses in up-to-date information.\n\n## How it works\n\n```\nUser Query → LLM → [Retrieve Relevant Docs] → LLM (with Context) → Answer\n```\n\n## Java Implementation with LangChain\n\nHere's a complete example using LangChain (the most common RAG implementation):\n\n```java\nimport org.langchain.core.client.Client;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.config.Config;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.document.Document;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.document.Passage;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.document.Retriever;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.document.SearchEngine;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.document.Store;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.document.Util;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.document.vectorstore.VectorStore;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.document.vectorstore.FaissVectorStore;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.document.vectorstore.FaissVectorStoreOptions;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.document.vectorstore.FaissVectorStoreOptionsBuilder;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.document.vectorstore.FaissVectorStoreOptionsBuilder.WithEmbeddingModel;\nimport org.langchain.core.client.document.vectorstore.FaissVectorStoreOptionsBuilder.WithIndexer;\n\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class RagExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // 1. Initialize the LLM client (uses OpenAI by default)\n        Client llmClient = new Client(\"openai\", \"gpt-4o\", \"api-key\");\n        \n        // 2. Create a vector store with FAISS indexer\n        // In practice, you'd load your own embedding model here\n        VectorStore vectorStore = new FaissVectorStore(\n            FaissVectorStoreOptions.builder()\n                .setEmbeddingModel(SomeEmbeddingModel.class) // e.g., OpenAI Embedding\n                .build()\n        );\n        \n        // 3. Add documents to the store (in production, these come from your database)\n        List<Document> documents = Arrays.asList(\n            Document.fromText(\"The Python programming language was created by Guido van Rossum...\"),\n            Document.fromText(\"Machine learning involves building systems that learn from data...\"),\n            Document.fromText(\"Natural language processing enables computers to understand human language...\")\n        );\n        \n        vectorStore.addDocuments(documents);\n        \n        // 4. Set up the retriever\n        Retriever retriever = new Retriever(vectorStore, new SearchEngine());\n        \n        // 5. Perform retrieval for a query\n        String query = \"Explain machine learning in simple terms\";\n        List<Passage> results = retriever.search(query, 3); // top 3 results\n        \n        System.out.println(\"Top results:\");\n        for (Passage p : results) {\n            System.out.println(p.getSource().getTitle() + \" -> \" + p.getContent());\n        }\n        \n        // 6. Generate answer using the LLM with retrieved context\n        String prompt = \"Answer the following question using only the provided context:\\n\\n\" +\n                        query + \"\\n\\nContext: \" + \n                        String.join(\"\\n\", results.stream()\n                            .map(r -> r.getSource().getTitle() + \": \") + r.getContent()));\n        \n        String answer = llmClient.runPrompt(llmClient.getPromptTemplate(), prompt);\n        System.out.println(\"Answer: \" + answer);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Key Components Explained\n\n| Component | Role |\n|-----------|------|\n| **Vector Store** | Stores document embeddings indexed for fast similarity search |\n| **Retriever** | Finds relevant passages given a query (uses FAISS, Chroma, etc.) |\n| **Generator** | The LLM that produces the final answer |\n\n## Important Considerations\n\n- **Retrieval quality matters**: Poor embeddings lead to irrelevant context\n- **Context length limits**: LLMs have token limits (~4096 for GPT-4o), so keep retrieved docs concise\n- **Freshness**: Periodically refresh your knowledge base to avoid stale information\n- **Cost**: Vector search adds infrastructure cost but improves accuracy over pure retrieval\n\nWould you like me to elaborate on any specific part (e.g., setting up a different vector store, handling multiple LLM models, or fine-tuning the retrieval strategy)?",
      "toolCalls" : [ ]
    }
  } ]
